            <ArticleTitle>Hysterosalpingo-contrast sonography versus radiographic hysterosalpingography in the evaluation of tubal patency.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>215-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ijgo.2009.02.001</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To compare the efficacy of hysterosalpingo-contrast sonography (HyCoSy) using a saline solution with that of radiographic hysterosalpingography (HSG) in the assessment of tubal patency.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">We compared the findings obtained by the 2 methods with those obtained by standard diagnostic laparoscopy with the chromopertubation test in a study with 88 infertile women younger than 40 years.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The sensitivity, specificity, and diagnostic accuracy of HyCoSy and HSG were similar, and 65 (74%) of the women reported the pain associated with HyCoSy as mild to moderate.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The HyCoSy procedure is well tolerated and can be used as a primary tool for the evaluation of tubal patency in infertile women.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
